            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Tech-Free or Tech Bound?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Comprehend descriptions in simple texts. Reading: Analyze informational texts for specific purposes. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by pronouncing the past tense verbs ending "ed."</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend descriptions in simple texts about technology use (e.g., "How does technology help us in school?").
Can analyze informational texts to understand the pros and cons of technology (e.g., "Digital technology makes communication faster and easier, but it can be distracting." "List two benefits of using technology.").
Can identify and pronounce past tense verbs in short texts, distinguishing between /d/, /ɪd/, and /t/ sounds (e.g., /d/ in "played," /ɪd/ in "started," and /t/ in "worked").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays two images on the board: one showing teens actively using various tech devices (cellphone, laptop) and another showing teens engaging in activities without technology (reading a book, playing sports, interacting face-to-face). Students are asked to briefly observe the images. Then, the teacher asks, "Which picture shows people who are 'tech-free'? Which shows people who are 'tech-bound'?" to activate prior knowledge related to the theme "Tech-Free or Tech Bound?".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly scan the images for key actions. For example, pointing to the tech-bound image, "Here, they are *using* their devices. They are very *connected*." Then pointing to the tech-free image, "Here, they *interact* directly. They look *relaxed*." The teacher highlights words like "use," "connected," "interact," and "relaxed" (vocabulary from Lesson 1) to connect them to the visuals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you *disconnect* from your cellphone for an hour, are you tech-free or tech-bound?" (Expected answer: Tech-free.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is checking social media *often* an example of being tech-free or tech-bound?" (Expected answer: Tech-bound.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher distributes a short informational text (approx. 150-200 words) titled "Digital Balance: The Pros and Cons of Our Devices." The text includes simple descriptions, comparative structures (e.g., "easier," "more difficult"), modal verbs (e.g., "we can learn," "you should reduce"), and past tense verbs ending with "-ed" (e.g., "helped," "distracted," "saved"). It incorporates vocabulary such as <i>technology, cellphone, internet, device, social media, benefits, use, reduce, disconnect, interact, helpful, distracted, focused</i>, <i>often, usually, actively</i>. (Genre: Informational text/blog post, Register: Semi-formal, Format: Printed text).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interprets and analyzes simple informational texts on familiar topics related to technology use, identifying main ideas and supporting details to understand the positive and negative aspects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Reading):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the text individually for the first time. The teacher asks, "What is the main topic of this text? Is it about *all* technology, or specifically about *how we use* technology?" (Expected answer: How we use technology / Digital Balance).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Specific Information (True/False):</b> Students read the text again to answer True or False questions about specific statements. For example:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Digital technology always makes communication *easier*." (T/F - depends on context in text)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Many students feel *distracted* when they use their *cellphones* during study time." (T/F)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We *could* reduce screen time by planning *meaningful* activities." (T/F)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Students match phrases from the text to complete sentences about "benefits" or "cons" of technology mentioned. E.g., Match "Digital technology makes communication..." to "...faster and easier."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension for specific details, identifying grammar structures (modals, comparatives, present perfect), and practicing phonemic awareness of past tense verbs ending "-ed."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Vocabulary and Grammar Cloze (Controlled Reading Practice):</b> The teacher provides a handout with sentences extracted from the reading text, but with key vocabulary words (e.g., <i>solution, benefits, focused, actively</i>) and grammar structures (e.g., 'can', 'should', 'easier') removed. Students read the incomplete sentences and fill in the blanks using a word bank provided, referring back to the original text for accuracy if needed.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "We ________ reduce our screen time by planning other activities." (Word bank: can, could, easier)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Using social media makes communication ________, but it can be distracting." (Word bank: easier, more difficult, helpful)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Identifying Past Tense Verbs & Pronunciation (Semi-controlled Reading Practice):</b> Students re-read the text specifically to locate and underline all past tense verbs ending in "-ed." The teacher then writes a few examples on the board (e.g., "helped," "distracted," "saved," "interacted") and models the three different pronunciations (/t/, /ɪd/, /d/). Students repeat chorally and individually, categorizing the verbs from the text under the correct pronunciation sound. This reinforces the "Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by pronouncing the past tense verbs ending 'ed'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pros & Cons Search (Pair Reading & Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students re-read the text to identify and list two "benefits" and two "cons" of technology use as described in the text. They must quote or paraphrase directly from the text. They then compare their lists with another pair, ensuring they have found the information and pronounced any identified past tense verbs ending "-ed" correctly when discussing their findings. This integrates "Discussing pros and cons."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Group Analysis & Presentation (Reading Application):</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a specific scenario card (e.g., "Using cellphones in class," "Spending all evening on social media," "Using the internet for school projects"). They must re-read the main text and use the information, vocabulary, and grammar to analyze their scenario. Specifically, they need to:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identify specific descriptions or ideas from the text that relate to their scenario.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Discuss the pros and cons of their scenario based on the reading, using phrases like, "I think technology is useful because it helps me learn," or "Digital technology makes communication faster and easier, but it can be distracting." (Pragmatic: Giving opinions, Discussing pros and cons)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Propose one "solution" or "suggestion" for their scenario using modals (e.g., "You could try using less screen time," "We should be more *focused*.") (Pragmatic: Offering suggestions).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Each group will prepare to briefly present their findings (focus on what they *read* and how it *applies*) to the class, referring to their text analysis.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> This activity directly addresses "Tech-Free or Tech Bound?" by requiring students to analyze and propose solutions for common technology-use scenarios based on their reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist & Peer Assessment.</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist to note group collaboration and use of target vocabulary/grammar during the group analysis and presentation. Peers also provide quick feedback using a simple checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group orally presents their analysis of their scenario card, explaining the pros and cons based on the text and offering a suggestion. They refer to specific parts of the text to support their points. This is primarily a reading comprehension assessment, but requires verbalizing the findings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehension: Accuracy in identifying pros/cons and descriptions from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary Use: Integration of at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>benefits, distracted, solution, reduce</i>) when discussing their analysis.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar Use: Correct use of modals (<i>can, could, should</i>) and comparatives (<i>easier, more difficult</i>) in their suggestions and discussions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Phonemic Awareness: Intelligible pronunciation of any past tense verbs ending "-ed" mentioned during their presentation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration: Active participation and respectful interaction within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting 'benefit' or 'con' of technology you read about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I now understand how to find specific information in a text to support an idea (Yes/No)."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-6 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did analyzing the text help me understand if being 'tech-free' or 'tech-bound' is better for different situations?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which new word or phrase from the text did I find most helpful today, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The activities effectively engaged students in reading for specific information and analyzing content. The phonemic awareness activity for "-ed" endings worked well to draw attention to pronunciation within the reading context. Some students struggled with distinguishing between main ideas and supporting details when listing pros and cons; I might need to provide more explicit sentence starters for textual evidence next time. The use of comparatives and modals in student output was present but could be more consistent. Providing a graphic organizer for the group analysis might scaffold the task more effectively for all learners, ensuring they extract and categorize information more systematically.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on the understanding of pros and cons of technology from this reading lesson. Students will use the ideas and vocabulary from today's text to express their opinions and engage in a guided debate about digital balance.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>